To make an OpenPGP key:

  1. Plug the YubiKey and a FAT USB stick labelled KEYS.
  2. Set the clock, as the Pi has no battery-backed one:
       date -u -s 'YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS'
  3. Run the key generator, /usr/bin/gpg-genkey:
       PASSPHRASE='...' gpg-genkey -n 'Your Name' -e you@example.org

The header comment of the script lists its options. The secret key is made
in memory, under /run/gnupg, which never swaps, and leaves only on the stick,
which systemd mounts on /mnt/usb when something reads it and unmounts after
10 seconds of disuse: wait that long before pulling it out.

To format a stick for the keys, find it with `blkid`: it is sda, and mmcblk0
is the SD card. This erases the stick:

  mkfs.fat -I -F 32 -n KEYS /dev/sda

To move the subkeys to the YubiKey, use `gpg --edit-key` and `keytocard`: the
keys stay in memory until the Pi powers off. `gpg --list-secret-keys`,
`gpg --card-status` and `ykman info` show what the keys and the YubiKey hold.
